  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-a-6-practice-problems-factoring-and-completing-the-square-2">
    <title>Factoring and completing the square</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>x^2+7x+12=(x+3)(x+4).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>2x^2-5x-3=(2x+1)(x-3).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^3-8=(x-2)(x^2+2x+4).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^2+10x+13=(x+5)^2-12.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>3x^2-12x+7=3(x-2)^2-5.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^2-4x-5=(x-5)(x+1),</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>x=5
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
x=-1.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-a-6-practice-problems-rational-expressions-2">
    <title>Rational expressions</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}
=
\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}
=
x+2,
\qquad x\neq2.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{x^2+3x+2}{x^2-1}
=
\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+1)}
=
\frac{x+2}{x-1},
\qquad x\neq -1,1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{1}{x}+\frac{1}{x+2}
=
\frac{x+2+x}{x(x+2)}
=
\frac{2x+2}{x(x+2)}
=
\frac{2(x+1)}{x(x+2)}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{\frac1{x+h}-\frac1x}{h}
=
\frac{\frac{x-(x+h)}{x(x+h)}}{h}
=
\frac{-h}{x(x+h)h}
=
-\frac1{x(x+h)}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{\sqrt{x+9}-3}{x}
\cdot
\frac{\sqrt{x+9}+3}{\sqrt{x+9}+3}
=
\frac{x}{x(\sqrt{x+9}+3)}
=
\frac1{\sqrt{x+9}+3},</md>
        <p>with the original restrictions <m>x\ge -9</m> and <m>x\neq0</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-a-6-practice-problems-exponents-and-radicals-2">
    <title>Exponents and radicals</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{1}{x^3\sqrt{x}}=x^{-7/2}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^{2/3}x^{5/3}=x^{7/3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^{3/2}=x\sqrt{x}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>(x^{-2})^3=x^{-6}=\frac1{x^6}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac1{\sqrt{x-4}}</md>
        <p>requires</p>
          <md>x-4&gt;0,</md>
        <p>so the domain is</p>
          <md>(4,\infty).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-a-6-practice-problems-inequalities-and-absolute-values-2">
    <title>Inequalities and absolute values</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>5-2x\le11</md>
        <p>gives</p>
          <md>-2x\le6.</md>
        <p>Divide by <m>-2</m> and reverse the inequality:</p>
          <md>x\ge -3.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>x^2+x-6=(x+3)(x-2).</md>
        <p>The product is negative between the roots, so</p>
          <md>-3&lt;x&lt;2.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The critical numbers are <m>-1</m> and <m>4</m>.  The quotient is positive when numerator and
denominator have the same sign, so</p>
          <md>(-\infty,-1)\cup(4,\infty).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>|x-3|&lt;0.5</md>
        <p>means</p>
          <md>2.5&lt;x&lt;3.5.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>|4x-1|\ge7</md>
        <p>means</p>
          <md>4x-1\le -7
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
4x-1\ge7.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>x\le -\frac32
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
x\ge2.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-a-6-practice-problems-sigma-notation-2">
    <title>Sigma notation</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{5}(k^2+1)
=
(1^2+1)+(2^2+1)+(3^2+1)+(4^2+1)+(5^2+1).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{20}k=\frac{20(21)}2=210.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{6}k^2=\frac{6(7)(13)}6=91.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sum_{k=0}^{5}2^k=\frac{1-2^6}{1-2}=63.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>On <m>[0,1]</m>,</p>
          <md>\Delta x=\frac1n,
\qquad
x_k=\frac{k}{n}.</md>
        <p>The right Riemann sum is</p>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left(\frac{k}{n}\right)^3\frac1n.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left(\frac{k}{n}\right)^3\frac1n
=
\frac1{n^4}\sum_{k=1}^{n}k^3.</md>
        <p>Using</p>
          <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n}k^3=\left(\frac{n(n+1)}2\right)^2,</md>
        <p>we get</p>
          <md>\frac1{n^4}\left(\frac{n(n+1)}2\right)^2
=
\frac{(n+1)^2}{4n^2}.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

